(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-pigmented-skin-lesions-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pigmented Skin Lesions Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a 7 mm naevus with an indistinct border and a pebbly surface, with tan and brown areas within it, on the upper back. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Dermoscopy","Wood's lamp","Serology","Biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic melanocytic naevus is diagnosed histologically. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with numerous solar lentigines asks what her lesions mean for her health. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The lesions will fade over the coming winter if she avoids further exposure to the sun","The lesions themselves are benign, but they mark sun damage that is also associated with actinic keratosis and skin cancers","The lesions are premalignant and each one carries a measurable chance of becoming a melanoma","The lesions indicate an underlying internal malignancy and warrant an urgent systemic screening of the whole body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the association includes squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma and melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has a 4 cm tan patch on the thigh, evenly pigmented like a café-au-lait spot, with about a dozen darker brown macules scattered within it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Naevus spilus","A large congenital melanocytic naevus","A cluster of agminated lentigines","A dysplastic melanocytic naevus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a spotted naevus, a variant of congenital naevus, most often on trunk and extremities. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man's family are alarmed by dozens of dark warty growths that appeared on his back over two years. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["They are benign, common in older adults, and the diagnosis is clinical without any need for biopsy","They are precancerous and each one carries a measurable chance of becoming a squamous cell carcinoma","They are a sign of an underlying internal malignancy and warrant urgent systemic investigation","They are contagious growths spread by contact and will need isolation until they are treated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy helps if a lesion becomes itchy or inflamed, though it may recur. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with fair skin, red hair and many freckles asks why he freckles when his darker-skinned friends do not. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["A variant of the MCR-1 receptor reduces the melanogenesis pathway and promotes pheomelanin, the yellow-red pigment","A variant of the TP53 tumour suppressor gene drives keratinocytic dysplasia in chronically sun-exposed skin","T-cell mediated destruction of the melanocytes removes pigment from discrete areas of the skin","A somatic mutation in melanocyte precursors produces pigmented lesions present from birth onwards"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — MCR-1 is the receptor for alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone, acting through cyclic adenosine monophosphate. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has one seborrheic keratosis on the back that has become itchy and inflamed where his belt rubs. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Cryotherapy, though the lesion may recur afterwards","Excision with negative margins to prevent it recurring","Topical depigmenting agents applied over several months","Observation alone, since no treatment exists for these"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — asymptomatic lesions need no treatment at all. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with several lentigines on the hands asks whether they need treating. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Topical depigmenting agents are the only option, since cryotherapy cannot be used at all","Sun protection alone will make the lesions fade over the course of the following winter","No treatment is necessary; cosmetic removal by cryotherapy or quality-switched laser is an option if she prefers","Excision with clear margins is needed on every single lesion, because of the risk of transformation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and the lesions are benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with dermatosis papulosa nigrans asks about removing the lesions. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Cryotherapy at the first visit is the treatment of choice, with laser reserved for recurrences","Topical depigmenting agents should be tried first, since the lesions are pigmented macules","Wide excision is needed because these lesions can transform into melanoma over time","They are best left untreated; excision, curettage or laser can be used, but cryotherapy is avoided"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy risks post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in this group. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with vitiligo has been using a topical corticosteroid on her eyelids for several months. Which risks should be checked for?","choices":["Teratogenicity and the need for pregnancy testing","Tooth staining and hepatotoxicity from the agent","Skin atrophy and raised intraocular pressure","Increased skin cancer risk and photosensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a topical calcineurin inhibitor suits areas where steroids cannot be applied. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has asymptomatic white non-scaly patches with sharp margins around the mouth, on the fingertips and in the genital area, appearing symmetrically over six months. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea versicolor of the face and trunk","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Non-segmental vitiligo","Segmental vitiligo"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — symmetrical distribution with a preference for face, genitals and acral areas. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman wants a mole on her back removed because her bra strap catches it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Removal is mandatory for any naevus subject to repeated friction from clothing","Removal for symptomatic or cosmetic reasons is reasonable, alongside sun protection counselling","Removal is not offered for symptoms, since a benign naevus should always be left alone","Removal must be preceded by a biopsy to confirm the lesion is not a melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and management is otherwise observation. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An 8-year-old girl has a solitary firm pink dome-shaped hairless papule 6 mm across on the cheek that grew over two months and has since been stable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Common acquired naevus","Blue naevus of the face","Spitz naevus","Pyogenic granuloma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a growth phase followed by a stable period, on face, neck, trunk or extremities. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with freckles asks whether laser treatment will get rid of them permanently. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["No treatment exists, so the lesions must simply be accepted as they are","Laser and intense pulsed light are preferred over cryotherapy, but the lesions can relapse","Laser removes the lesions permanently, so no further sun protection will be needed","Cryotherapy is the preferred option because it treats each lesion individually"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — sun protection and education remain the foundation of management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A newborn has a large congenital melanocytic naevus over the posterior midline of the neck and scalp. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, with or without total spine, matched to the location of the naevus","Excision of the lesion in the neonatal period before any imaging is arranged","Observation with clinical photographs, repeating the whole assessment at twelve months of age","Skin biopsy of the lesion to confirm that it is melanocytic in its origin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the concern is neurocutaneous melanosis. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with freckles across her cheeks asks what will reduce them. Which is the most appropriate treatment plan?","choices":["Cryotherapy to each lesion with five to ten second freezes, repeated over several sessions as needed","Excision with negative margins for the largest lesions, with observation of the remainder","Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy given three times weekly over a five week course","Sun protection and counselling first, then topical depigmenting agents, with intense pulsed light or laser if needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids and botanicals are the topical options. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man who has worked outdoors for decades has light to dark brown macules with irregular borders on the shoulders, some coalescing where he recalls a severe sunburn. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ephelides","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans","Solar lentigines","Lentigo simplex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they coalesce at sites of severe sunburn and 90% of people have them by fifty. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 2-year-old with a large congenital melanocytic naevus is being considered for surgery, but the surgeon reports very little uninvolved skin available for grafting. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Use cryotherapy in place of excision, since it needs no donor skin for grafting","Defer any decision until the child reaches adolescence and then reassess it","Observation may be the better option, since graft site availability is part of the decision","Proceed with excision regardless, since melanoma risk always outweighs graft considerations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — surgery is ideal where feasible, but the decision depends on every patient. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with naevus spilus is found to have a vascular anomaly on further examination. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Naevus spilus can be associated with anomalies of vascular, central nervous system or connective tissue origin","The vascular anomaly is unrelated, since naevus spilus has no described systemic associations at all","The vascular anomaly indicates neurocutaneous melanosis and brain imaging is needed","The vascular anomaly indicates a familial cancer syndrome and genetic testing is needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion itself rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with red hair and fair skin has small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across over her nose and cheeks. Her mother says they are darker every summer and almost invisible by February. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ephelides","Lentigo simplex","Solar lentigines","Naevus spilus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the seasonal fading is what separates them from lentigines, which do not fade. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 39-year-old Polynesian woman with facial papules asks why her doctor will not freeze them off as a friend had done elsewhere. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Cryotherapy is avoided because it would spread the lesions across the rest of the face","Cryotherapy is avoided in this condition because of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Cryotherapy is avoided because the lesions are too small for it to be practical at all","Cryotherapy is avoided because it would risk transforming the lesions into melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — excision, curettage or laser can be used where treatment is wanted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has vitiligo affecting roughly 20% of his body surface. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Phototherapy with narrow band ultraviolet B, ideally combined with topical therapy","Topical corticosteroid alone applied twice daily to all the affected areas","Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented areas","PUVA phototherapy, which is preferred to narrow band ultraviolet B here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred over PUVA, which raises skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man asks why he has more moles than his sister when they grew up in the same house. Which risk factors are named?","choices":["Older age, cumulative sun damage, tanning and use of hormonal contraception","Ultraviolet radiation exposure, male sex and a genetic component, with more lesions in light skin that sunburns","Ultraviolet radiation exposure, female sex and a purely environmental cause, with more lesions in darker skin","Autoimmune disease, immunosuppression and a family history of thyroid or coeliac disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — numbers peak in the thirties then decline. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has several uniformly brown round macules 3 to 4 mm across, some on the forearms and some on the buttocks, unchanged through the winter. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ephelides","Dysplastic naevi","Blue naevi","Lentigines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas and do not fade. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a biopsy-confirmed pigmented spindle cell naevus of the thigh. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Sun protection counselling alone","Excision with negative margins","Observation with annual photographs","Cryotherapy for cosmetic improvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lesion is benign but excision with clear margins is the described management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with newly diagnosed vitiligo is having her workup arranged. Which investigations are appropriate?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation of a skin scraping taken from the affected patches","Wood's lamp examination in a dark room, with a complete blood count and antinuclear antibody","Punch biopsy of the leading edge sent for direct immunofluorescence and antigen mapping","Minimal erythema dose testing to ultraviolet A and to ultraviolet B before treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the labs address the autoimmune diseases associated with vitiligo. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with numerous dysplastic naevi asks what he should do between clinic visits. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Have every naevus excised over the coming year to eliminate the melanoma risk","Avoid all sun exposure entirely, since photoprotection alone will clear the naevi","Apply a topical depigmenting agent to the naevi to lighten their appearance","Use sun protection and report any lesion that changes or is newly developing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — management is observation, biopsy of changing lesions and excision where melanoma is a concern. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has a small congenital melanocytic naevus on the forearm with no neurological symptoms. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Cryotherapy of the lesion, since it removes pigmented lesions without leaving a scar","Observation, since management balances melanoma risk with cosmetic and functional considerations","Immediate excision, since every congenital naevus carries an equally high melanoma risk","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, since all congenital naevi warrant it at diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — smaller lesions carry lower melanoma risk than larger ones. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with a stable 5 mm blue macule on the scalp asks whether it needs removing. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Excision is required now, since blue naevi have a high malignant potential","Cryotherapy is appropriate, since it clears deeply pigmented dermal lesions","Biopsy is required now, since all blue naevi are diagnosed histologically","Observation is appropriate, with biopsy or excision only if changes are noted"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — small lesions are diagnosed clinically. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has a single deep blue-black macule 6 mm across on the dorsum of the hand, present for two years and unchanged. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A cellular blue naevus","A pigmented spindle cell naevus","A dysplastic melanocytic naevus","A common blue naevus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — under 1 cm, deeply pigmented, arising in adolescence, on dorsal hands, scalp, buttocks or sacrum. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man who had photochemotherapy for psoriasis has brown-black irregular macules on his buttocks as well as his forearms. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Ephelides, which appear on sun-exposed skin in fair-skinned individuals","Naevus spilus, which is present at birth or within the first years of life","PUVA lentigines, which affect sun-protected as well as sun-exposed sites","Ordinary solar lentigines, which affect only chronically sun-exposed sites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they relate to the total number of treatments, male sex, fair skin and older age. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
